#{[17592186045463 #edb/ref 10 :activity.type/lesson #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045485 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/easy #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045486 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/hard #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045487 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/moderate #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045492 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/blank #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045493 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/radio #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045498 #edb/ref 10 :answer.type/math #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1003 #edb/ref 17592186046659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1007 "Calculus II" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1008 "Our Calculus II course builds upon the differential calculus covered in Calculus I , providing in-depth coverage of integral calculus including integration techniques, applications of integration. Advanced topics such as series convergence tests, Taylor series, calculus with parametric and polar equations, and introductory differential equations are also covered. This comprehensive course will prepare students for further studies in advanced mathematics, engineering, statistics, machine learning, and other fields requiring a solid foundation in integral calculus." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1009 "Further your understanding of calculus: master advanced integration techniques, model real-world situations using differential equations, and more." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1012 106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1013 "CA2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1014 #uuid "4965f9cc-fd21-5d50-b441-6e80983b17dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048539 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048539 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048539 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048539 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048539 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048539 #edb/ref 1031 "Volumes of Revolution Using the Disc Method: Rotation About Other Axes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048539 #edb/ref 1033 1677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048539 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "a8385b0e-c407-5162-b441-31d369d21310" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048643 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048643 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048643 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048643 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048643 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048643 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048643 #edb/ref 1031 "Volumes of Revolution Using the Washer Method: Rotation About the Coordinate Axes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048643 #edb/ref 1033 414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048643 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "b16c2962-e115-56a0-9029-0d281a36443f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049885 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049885 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049885 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049885 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049885 #edb/ref 1031 "Volumes of Revolution Using the Washer Method: Rotation About Other Axes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049885 #edb/ref 1033 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049885 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "1b27fe0a-58ff-57f2-8e77-9341dd7fc153" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095183 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186535487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095183 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186535504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095183 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186535516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095183 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186535591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095183 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186535645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095183 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095183 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095183 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186535645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095183 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6a5fce8d-1e83-5220-b88b-1bc28cf4c002" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095303 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186535606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095303 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186535655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095303 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186535753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095303 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186535754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095303 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186535761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095303 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095303 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095303 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186535655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095303 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e4b658f2-ac3d-5bc5-8869-0fed4b76530f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096531 #edb/ref 1069 "e-593" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096531 #edb/ref 1071 "The region below enclosed by the curves $y=\\sqrt x$ and $y=x$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $y=1.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid. \n\n![](images/36/362a92640ed68dfd5fa85f0338d95233003e9f8a1b059a5dffa2cda243a1411b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096531 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b6dd2970-3cdc-58fc-8f28-0bfdcd738d6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096531 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n \t\tx & =\\sqrt x  \\[3pt]\n \t\tx-\\sqrt x & = 0 \\[3pt]\n \t\t\\sqrt x \\left(\\sqrt x - 1\\right) &= 0 \\[3pt]\n \t\t\tx& = 0, 1\n\t}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/54/5456bed18c03e823c8e9388571b5671f5f476f5caa1085793314b57080b2b07c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ and\n\n- *leaves* though the outer curver $y=x.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=x.$\n\n![](images/51/5136903876ed5ea811ebf28e0da905b5029b2c9311527fce9d0fed8e7bd1dd46.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(1)-(x)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1-x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n![](images/c8/c850643bb1c315d0516eff55d6270078504c1c98687cfac40e0c9b4b2c771b7c.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(1)-(\\sqrt{x})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1-\\sqrt{x}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nV &= {\\color{blue}V_{\\text{outer}}} - {\\color{brown}V_{\\text{inner}}} \\[5pt]\n \t\t& = \\pi \\int_0^1 [1-x]^2 - \\pi \\int_0^1 [1-\\sqrt x]^2\\,\\textrm d x \\[5pt]\n \t\t& = \\pi \\int_0^1 \\left((1-x)^2 - (1-\\sqrt x)^2\\right) \\,\\textrm d x \\[5pt]\n \t\t& = \\pi \\int_0^1 (x^2-3x+2\\sqrt x) \\,\\,\\textrm d x \\[5pt]\n \t\t& = \\pi\\left. \\left( \\dfrac {1} 3 x^3 - \\dfrac{3}{2} x^2 + \\dfrac{4}{3} x^{3/2 } \\ \\right)\\right|_0^1\\[5pt] \n \t\t& =  \\pi \\left[\\left( \\dfrac {1}3 (1)^3 - \\dfrac{3}{2}(1)^2  + \\dfrac{4 }{3}(1)^{3/2 } \\right) - (0) \\right]\\[5pt]\n \t\t& =  \\pi \\left(\\dfrac{1}3 - \\dfrac{3}{2} +\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\\[5pt]\n \t\t& = \\dfrac {\\pi}{6} .\n \t\t}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096532 #edb/ref 1069 "e-594" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096532 #edb/ref 1071 "The region enclosed by the curves $x=y^2$ and $x=2-y^2$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $x=-1.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid.\n\n![](images/e8/e8f0f999521058f3213e11bd3df67a95a6ea8604e6853d06923ffbba6cf9d17c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096532 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b79b0d88-0b42-5223-927b-5a4023f6c329" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096532 #edb/ref 1074 "First, to determine the limits of integration, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =2-{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=-1$ to $y=1.$\n\nNext, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/c3/c3c9217600a26e11614752b2ca2ac8b3aa6c12b0df9f3a8c13d2b32c5b338107.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=y^2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* though the outer curve $x={2-y^2}.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of outer and inner solids.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x={2-y^2}.$\n\n![](images/36/3696bac064f5157b5c4ab3aefe34128d2d126a0a6b586106eb1e439bc90e7df5.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[\\,(2-{y}^{2})-(-1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[3-{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=y^2.$\n\n![](images/f5/f59ee5966f0cb9572d00e7f7278a7462841aef6b18f4865924303ad65b50738c.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,({y}^{2})-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[{y}^{2}+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[3-{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[{y}^{2}+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}({[3-{y}^{2}]}^{2}-{[{y}^{2}+1]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(8-8{y}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(8y-\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{3})|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(8-\\frac{8}{3})-(-8+\\frac{8}{3})] \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{32}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096569 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7508" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096569 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a8/a8a9dd6c0bd31ceb221d0d72e6f0238ab51760cd9a32ba92bb8bdc85429a899f.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096569 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8e436e4c-45da-528c-885b-9a094919a652" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096569 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/59/59041e6817cbf26f9de00de0ec864cea57f1a419877fe5c3203857c5488c7324.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=-2x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* though the outer curver $y=2x^2.$\n\nNow, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=2x^2.$\n\n![](images/07/0761073a5f7f417a07f35cb0b7c08dd0b4ea6bd84da71f3e474911ee036c546b.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[\\,(3)-(2{x}^{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[3-2{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=-2x.$\n\n![](images/9e/9ea5e5dd50dcd623532557e5365118ae5c9b950be059ba8c0069c7b36071f2c3.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[\\,(3)-(-2x)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[3+2x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[3-2{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[3+2x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}({[3-2{x}^{2}]}^{2}-{[3+2x]}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096570 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7509" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096570 #edb/ref 1071 "The region enclosed by the curves $x=-y^2$ and $x = -y$ is rotated $360^\\circ$ about the line $x={1}.$ Find an integral that gives the volume of the resulting solid.\n\n![](images/64/6471c937fb79a2266188668ebdb698eb926655b6002f8f259410b96efcb15247.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096570 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d064d066-3bbe-5381-ab56-3fc407da144f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096570 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2} & =-y \\\\ {y}^{2}-y & =0 \\\\ y(y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1.$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/4d/4da6108728a226e19cea708a09381c9054b129590d2a6d2c07fbfacdc30367a5.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=-{y}^2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* though the outer curve $x=-y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=-y.$\n\n![](images/02/02798e08e1eb8cae41e4754cfed267401298d454ce2ef856f47f2ae87a8543ee.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\,(1)-(-y)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1+y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=-{y}^2.$\n\n![](images/5c/5c5e2060b1321856349719987e43b3424d49b465255295b8777002d85cbe6792.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\,(1)-(-{y}^{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1+{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1+y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1+{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[1+y]}^{2}-{[1+{y}^{2}]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096729 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096729 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096729 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096729 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096729 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535983 #edb/ref 1759218