y Rotation About a Horizontal Line" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096764 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186096569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096764 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e9016e61-3365-5f53-ae10-cc94e9c638f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096765 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096765 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096765 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096765 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096765 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096765 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096765 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096765 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096765 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096765 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186096570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096765 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "26659894-bc6d-50fe-a5f8-029c962452cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096871 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096871 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096871 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096871 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096871 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096871 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096871 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096871 #edb/ref 1045 "Volumes of Revolution Using the Washer Method: Rotation About Other Axes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096871 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186097608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096871 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096871 #edb/ref 1050 1380.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096871 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ccadebeb-ab4f-5e8c-b59d-112db894f229" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097209 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097209 #edb/ref 1069 "q-30118" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097209 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097209 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c6/c62793a0dc7ff37a4d6b7cf157239f21e6b0cdef8c3d428916f074584462598b.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=3x$ for $y\\in [0,9]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=5.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097209 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "89556ba4-a6e5-533a-b6c1-5971b5251496" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097209 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097209 #edb/ref 1074 "First, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equations of our curves for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y} \\\\ y & =3x\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{3}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/bd/bd75a7448cfda8237a403f4ea67a99c38e41788839f5c88b89487106e49547d3.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=\\sqrt{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=\\frac{1}{3}y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=\\frac{1}{3}y.$\n\n![](images/0e/0ec13c4447954916af6e3b72abffefb69a46a12671b7b8388a873835654ec921.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{9}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{9}{[\\,(5)-(\\frac{1}{3}y)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{9}{[5-\\frac{1}{3}y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=\\sqrt{y}.$\n\n![](images/74/7404d889d1388540821bbd282f82322e3249534b8d4518a6236e9f4bf3164d2c.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{9}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{9}[\\,(5)-(\\sqrt{y})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{9}[5-\\sqrt{y}]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{9}({(5-\\frac{1}{3}y)}^{2}-{(5-\\sqrt{y})}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{9}(\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{2}-\\frac{13}{3}y+10\\sqrt{y})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{20{y}^{3/2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{27}-\\frac{13{y}^{2}}{6})|}_{0}^{9} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{20\\cdot {9}^{3/2}}{3}+\\frac{{9}^{3}}{27}-\\frac{13\\cdot {9}^{2}}{6})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{63\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097229 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097229 #edb/ref 1069 "q-3385" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097229 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097229 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e8/e8d2665ab162ff21e358a80a6e42659b0385244a08d94f8de3ad5df12b434a9a.png)\n\nThe region above enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=\\sqrt{x}$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=1.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097229 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5ae9fb77-cc16-5042-8460-37410a7d25d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097229 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097229 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\sqrt{x} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x}({x}^{3/2}-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/7a/7aaa03f21130529bdf5572acac29bfe7566724d8acacc99ca5fc0b035de0398b.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/a7/a7bd5b7f8e61be991cb6a54e9b1277bd6df4fd874dbd72ec90802b4f957c31bd.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(1)-({x}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n![](images/ee/ee93f00e178fa7fd7b8702538298a15a5c98d94205deeb71401a49d8dd891545.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(1)-(\\sqrt{x})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1-\\sqrt{x}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1-\\sqrt{x}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}((1-{x}^{2})^{2}-(1-\\sqrt{x})^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-x+2\\sqrt{x})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{1}{5}\\,{x}^{5}-\\frac{2}{3}\\,{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}\\,{x}^{3/2}\\text{ })|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi ((\\frac{1}{5}\\,(1)^{5}-\\frac{2}{3}\\,(1)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,(1)^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}\\,(1)^{3/2})-(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{11}{30}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097608 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097608 #edb/ref 1061 638288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097608 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "626bee34-cf24-50f9-b6b3-1fcbce8fe1a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097608 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097609 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097609 #edb/ref 1061 638292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097609 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "52be5c09-e883-51da-a981-64e4b7986356" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097609 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097610 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097610 #edb/ref 1061 638295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097610 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ca0b12a1-0e89-5062-b87e-358443f928e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097610 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097611 #edb/ref 1061 638299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097611 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bb053558-c7a0-533b-bcf7-e8427cfa707a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097611 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097659 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097659 #edb/ref 1061 638289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097659 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "38143ed9-ca20-5c45-b750-274568e87032" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097659 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097660 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097660 #edb/ref 1061 638296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097660 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3a272f90-abf1-569a-853d-f9c7cfbd0534" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097660 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097796 #edb/ref 1064 "The region enclosed by the curves $y=x^4$ and $y=x$ is shown below. Suppose that this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the horizontal line $y=-1,$ generating a solid of revolution. How can we write an integral expression that gives the volume of the solid?\n\n![](images/18/1880138e4a20cb5a47ba89cb3177d5dc6e74f998d7fdd428cdded7af38cadd02.png)\n\nFirst, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4} & =x \\\\ {x}^{4}-x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{3}-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/49/49da0885bbf23e586b488e2b2a9bcaa0d923d3591833c9b8beb31a611c1d8b43.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=x^4,$ and\n\n- *leaves* though the outer curve $y=x.$\n\nNow, we can express the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves, as follows:\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=x.$\n\n![](images/35/352ff26bb5dce0a923c102c4528bd89555b49203c212fb73a42cf31cdf8b226e.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(x)-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[x+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=x^4.$\n\n![](images/ee/ee0fa31b61acd4a1f2a9b6bb21dcd0e5eed3c529780fcb67096d40dccf90c20c.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\,({x}^{4})-(-1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{4}+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[x+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{4}+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[x+1]}^{2}-{[{x}^{4}+1]}^{2})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097796 #edb/ref 1065 1064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097796 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8e33de60-9df4-540e-b19b-f12088b349e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097796 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097797 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, suppose that the region enclosed by the curves $y=\\dfrac x2$ and $y=\\sqrt x$ is rotated $360^\\circ$ about the vertical line $x=-2.$ Let's find the volume of the resulting solid.\n\n![](images/78/7876211e2781a079ac2444436e671e2d61c28ec09774c0acad65e96fb537927a.png)\n\nFirst, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equations of our curves for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{x}{2} & \\,⟹\\,x=2y \\\\ y=\\sqrt{x} & \\,⟹\\,x={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y & ={y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y & =0 \\\\ y(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y={2}.$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/c3/c3fd84335240208fb158da9b67aaffa07cf490c67f8a77bf28947df998180b76.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x={y^2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* though the outer curve $x=2y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=2y.$\n\n![](images/6a/6a075f9b5fb64923de7037b31ec6dd0408e88aa6d965ebb8b204b6c560e23ec1.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,(2y)-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[2y+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=y^2.$\n\n![](images/cc/cca4c498956a16cb374a8d0b3341a09e52844026593557710525d07be79a233a.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,({y}^{2})-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[{y}^{2}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[2y+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[{y}^{2}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({[2y+2]}^{2}-{[{y}^{2}+2]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(-{y}^{4}+8y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}+4{y}^{2})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi [(-\\frac{32}{5}+16)-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{48\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097797 #e